      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High-glycemic index (GI) foods cause a rapid rise in blood sugar and insulin levels after consumption, while low-GI foods result in a slower and more steady release of sugar, leading to less insulin response. Consuming high-GI foods can lead to spikes in blood sugar, which can be problematic for people with diabetes or insulin resistance. On the other hand, low-GI foods help maintain a steady blood sugar level, supporting better blood sugar and insulin management over time.",
